 ],
 "literal": "On that day's moon, in that white moonlight, / we had our father too, and others did not take our hill. / On this day's moon, in this white moonlight, / the kings with victorious, beaten drums, our / hill too they took, and we do not have our father.",
 "translation": "On that moonlit night, in that white moonlight, we had our father, and others had not taken our hill. On this moonlit night, in this white moonlight, the kings with victorious, beating drums have taken our hill, and we no longer have our father.",
 "modern": "அன்றைய திங்கள் நாளில், அந்த வெண்மையான நிலவொளியில், எங்களோடு எங்கள் தந்தையும் இருந்தார்; எங்கள் மலையையும் பிறர் கைப்பற்றவில்லை. இன்றைய திங்கள் நாளில், இந்த வெண்மையான நிலவொளியில், வெற்றி முரசு கொட்டும் மூவேந்தர்கள் எங்கள் மலையையும் கைப்பற்றிக் கொண்டனர்; நாங்களோ எங்கள் தந்தையையும் இழந்து நிற்கிறோம்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is a deeply moving lament (kaiyaṟu nilai) sung by the daughters of the generous chieftain Pari after his tragic death. The three crowned kings of the Tamil land laid siege to Pari's Parambu hill, killed him, and seized his territory. The daughters contrast their happy past with their desolate present using the imagery of the moon. Just one month ago, under the same bright white moonlight, they had both their loving father and their secure mountain home. Now, under the very same moonlight, they have lost everything—their land has been seized by victorious kings, and they are left orphaned and helpless.",
